  "preamble": "Spirantization of a uvular stop to a velar fricative. In Georgian the Old Georgian uvular stop written ჴ (qar), described as /q/ or /qʰ/, merged with the velar fricative ხ /x/ and the letter fell out of use (ჴელი qeli > ხელი xeli 'hand', ჴმაჲ qmay > ხმა xma 'voice'). The Zan languages (Mingrelian and Laz) show the same spirantization directly from Proto-Georgian-Zan *q (*qar- 'ox' > Laz, Mingrelian xoǯi; Old Georgian ჴარი qari), while Svan still keeps ჴ q. In Azerbaijani the Turkic back-vowel stop [q] became [x] in syllable codas: Old Anatolian Turkish çoq gave çox 'much', and words still spelled with q are pronounced with [x] finally (ayaq [ɑˈjɑx] 'foot'), while in onsets the same sound became /g/. Spirantizations that end in a uvular fricative, such as Chuvash хура 'black' and Mongolian хар [χar], belong to the q > χ cell.",
